0253 | 	If A is a square matrix of order n and $\alpha\subseteq \left\{1,2,..n\right\}$ then $A[\alpha]$ will denote the sub matrix of A contained in the intersection of the rows and columns indexed by $\alpha$. Newton's inequalities have been shown to hold for Hermitian and M Matrices \cite[Theorem 1]{OH}. If A is either a non-negative definite Hermitian matrix  or a M Matrix and $\beta\subseteq\left\{1,2,..n\right\}$then the determinant   of A satisfies:
0254 | 	$\det A[\alpha] \det A[\beta] \geq \det A[\alpha\cap\beta]\det A[\alpha\cup\beta]$. If F is a real valued function then for $1\leq k \leq n$ define $S^F_k=\frac{1}{(^n_k)} \sum_{\alpha\subseteq\left\{1,2,..n\right\},k=\left|\alpha\right|} F(\alpha)$. We will show that if F satisfies $F(\alpha\cap\beta)F(\alpha\cup\beta)\leq F(\alpha)F(\beta)$ for all $\alpha$ and $\beta$ that are subsets of $\left\{1,2,..n\right\}$ with  $F(\phi)=1$ then $(S^F_k)^2\geq S^F_{k-1} S^F_{k+1}$.
